---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const lang = 'en-us';
const post = {
  title: 'Thermal Spas Between Ischia and Sorrento',
  subtitle: "Ischia's thermal parks, the sulphur springs of Scrajo in Vico Equense and the Ulysse spa in Sorrento: seasons, 2026 prices, how to get there and which one suits you",
  category: 'GUIDES',
  date: 'October 24, 2026',
  readTime: '12 min',
  image: '/images/terme-spa-hero.webp',
  imageAlt: "A swimmer resting in an indoor spa pool with a garden behind the glass",
  path: '/en-us/blog/thermal-spas-ischia-sorrento/',
};

const metaTitle = 'Thermal Spas Ischia and Sorrento: Prices and Seasons 2026';
const metaDescription = "Ischia's thermal parks, the Scrajo sulphur springs in Vico Equense and the Ulysse spa in Sorrento: 2026 prices, seasons and how to get there from Sorrento.";

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/en-us/' },
  { name: 'Blog', url: '/en-us/blog/' },
  { name: 'Thermal Spas Between Ischia and Sorrento', url: '/en-us/blog/thermal-spas-ischia-sorrento/' },
];

const quickInfo = [
  { label: 'Largest park', value: 'Poseidon, Forio: 20 pools' },
  { label: 'Poseidon 2026', value: '50 euros full day, open to 31 October' },
  { label: 'Negombo 2026', value: '55 to 70 euros full day, closed 13 October' },
  { label: 'Ischia season', value: 'Roughly late April to October' },
  { label: 'Sorrento to Ischia', value: 'Direct hydrofoil, about 1 hour, April to October' },
  { label: 'Scrajo', value: 'Cool sulphur pool, Vico Equense, summer only' },
  { label: 'Year round option', value: 'Ulysse spa, Sorrento (indoor)' },
  { label: 'Always bring', value: 'Swim cap and non slip sandals' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Which thermal park on Ischia is best?',
    a: 'It depends on what you want. Poseidon in Forio is the largest, with 20 pools, a long beach and the longest season. Negombo in Lacco Ameno has the best garden and a sandy bay, but it is the most expensive. Castiglione and Aphrodite Apollon cost less and are quieter, and Cavascura is a rustic historic bathhouse rather than a park.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much does a day at an Ischia thermal park cost?',
    a: 'In 2026 a full day cost 50 euros at Poseidon, 55 to 70 euros at Negombo depending on the season, about 40 euros at Aphrodite Apollon and about 28 euros at Castiglione. Afternoon tickets are cheaper everywhere. Prices change every season, so check the official site before you go.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I visit Ischia thermal parks as a day trip from Sorrento?',
    a: 'Yes, between April and October, when a direct hydrofoil runs from Sorrento to Ischia in about an hour on some days of the week. Check the return time before you leave. Out of season you go through Naples, which turns it into a long day.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are the thermal parks open in winter?',
    a: 'No. The big parks open from around late April and close in October: in 2026 Negombo closed on 13 October and Poseidon closes on 31 October. Castiglione has already closed and will publish its 2027 dates. In winter, the indoor Ulysse spa in Sorrento is the practical option nearby.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Scrajo in Vico Equense a hot spring?',
    a: 'No. The sulphur water at Scrajo comes out of the rock at roughly 18 degrees, so it is a cool, mineral pool by the sea rather than a hot bath. It is used from spring to early autumn, together with the beach club built around it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can children go into the thermal pools?',
    a: "Rules vary. Poseidon does not allow under 12s in the thermal pools but has seawater pools and a beach for them, with children under 4 free and 4 to 11 at half price. Negombo prices children by height: under 1 metre is free. Hot pools are not recommended for small children anywhere.",
  },
];

const sidebarLinks = [
  { label: 'Ischia', href: '/en-us/islands/ischia/' },
  { label: 'Forio', href: '/en-us/islands/forio/' },
  { label: 'Maronti Beach', href: '/en-us/beaches/islands/spiaggia-maronti/' },
  { label: 'Vico Equense', href: '/en-us/sorrento-peninsula/vico-equense/' },
  { label: 'Sorrento', href: '/en-us/sorrento-peninsula/sorrento/' },
  { label: 'Ferries Guide', href: '/en-us/guide/ferries/' },
];

const authorRole = 'Local Expert · Amalfi Coast';

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Capri vs Ischia',
    category: 'GUIDES',
    date: 'June 4, 2026',
    image: '/images/capri-vs-ischia-hero.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Capri and Ischia compared',
    path: '/en-us/blog/capri-vs-ischia/'
  },
  {
    title: 'Procida vs Ischia',
    category: 'GUIDES',
    date: 'June 19, 2026',
    image: '/images/procida-vs-ischia-hero.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Procida and Ischia compared',
    path: '/en-us/blog/procida-vs-ischia/'
  }
];

const keyTakeaways = [
  'Ischia has the thermal parks: Poseidon in Forio, Negombo in Lacco Ameno, Castiglione in Casamicciola, Aphrodite Apollon and Cavascura near Sant\'Angelo. They are seasonal, roughly late April to October.',
  'In 2026 a full day cost 50 euros at Poseidon and 55 to 70 euros at Negombo. Castiglione and Aphrodite Apollon are the cheaper parks. Prices change every year, so treat these as a guide.',
  'Scrajo in Vico Equense is the historic sulphur spring of the Sorrento Peninsula. The water is cool, around 18 degrees, and today you bathe in it at a beach club open from spring to early autumn.',
  'The Ulysse spa in Sorrento is an indoor wellness centre with a warm saltwater pool, sauna and steam room. It is not a natural spring, but it is the option that works in winter.',
  'From Sorrento, a direct hydrofoil reaches Ischia in about an hour from April to October, on some days of the week only. Vico Equense is a short Circumvesuviana ride away.',
];
---

<BlogPostLayout
  lang={lang}
  post={post}
  metaTitle={metaTitle}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  quickInfo={quickInfo}
  faq={faq}
  sidebarLinks={sidebarLinks}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  relatedArticles={relatedArticles}
  pageKey="thermal-spas-ischia-sorrento"
>

  <!-- Key Takeaways -->
  <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white p-8 rounded-[2rem]">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
      <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-med-gold flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-lg font-bold text-med-gold uppercase tracking-[0.15em]">Key Takeaways</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="space-y-4">
      {keyTakeaways.map((point) => (
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-med-gold flex-shrink-0 mt-2"></span>
          <span class="text-white/80 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">{point}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Intro -->
  <div>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-xl leading-relaxed mb-6 font-light">
      The Gulf of Naples sits on volcanic ground, and the warm water that comes with it has been used for bathing since Greek and Roman times. Most of it surfaces on <a href="/en-us/islands/ischia/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Ischia</a>, where hot springs feed a string of thermal parks. On the Sorrento Peninsula there is one historic spring, the sulphur water of Scrajo in <a href="/en-us/sorrento-peninsula/vico-equense/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Vico Equense</a>, and in <a href="/en-us/sorrento-peninsula/sorrento/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Sorrento</a> itself there is an indoor spa that stays open when the islands close. Here is what each one offers, what it cost in 2026 and how to reach it from Sorrento or Positano.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Ischia -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Ischia: The Thermal Parks</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      A thermal park on Ischia is a garden with a series of pools at different temperatures, fed by the island's springs, usually alongside seawater pools, a beach, a restaurant and a treatment centre. You pay once at the gate and spend the day moving between them. All the big parks are seasonal: they open in April and close in October, and the 2026 season is already winding down.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Giardini Poseidon Terme, Forio</h3>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The largest park on the island, on the Citara bay in <a href="/en-us/islands/forio/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Forio</a>. It has 20 thermal and seawater pools on six hectares of terraced gardens, about 500 metres of sandy beach, three restaurants and a wellness centre with a steam grotto, a Turkish bath and a Japanese bath. The 2026 season runs from <strong>18 April to 31 October</strong>, from 9:00 to 19:00 (18:00 from 1 October), so it is the one still open as autumn arrives.
    </p>
    <ul class="space-y-3 mb-8">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>2026 prices.</strong> 50 euros for a full day, 45 euros for the afternoon (from 13:00), plus a 5 euro refundable deposit for the wristband. Multi day passes bring the daily price down.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Children.</strong> Under 4 enter free and 4 to 11 pay half. Under 12s are not allowed in the thermal pools, but have three seawater pools and the beach.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Rules.</strong> A swim cap is required in all pools, and pools start emptying half an hour before closing.</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Parco Termale Negombo, Lacco Ameno</h3>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Negombo fills the San Montano bay in Lacco Ameno with more than nine hectares of botanical garden, a sandy beach, about a dozen thermal and seawater pools and a wellness centre. It is the most expensive park and the one people describe for the garden as much as the water. The 2026 season ran from <strong>23 April to 13 October</strong>, so it is now closed until next spring.
    </p>
    <ul class="space-y-3 mb-8">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>2026 prices.</strong> In high season (3 July to 14 September) about 70 euros for a full day, 50 for a half day and 35 for the late afternoon. Outside those weeks about 55, 40 and 30 euros.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Children.</strong> Priced by height: under 1 metre free, between 1 and 1.40 metres a reduced rate, taller children pay as adults.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Booking.</strong> Tickets can be booked online, which is worth doing for summer weekends. Towels and bathrobes can be rented.</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Parco Termale Castiglione, Casamicciola</h3>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      A smaller park on the north coast, with indoor and outdoor pools above the sea, each fed by thermal water at a different temperature, plus a spa, a restaurant and its own hotel. It is one of the cheapest options: in 2026 adult entry cost about <strong>28 euros</strong> (29 in August), with children from 2 to 12 at 10 euros (17.50 in July and August). The park has closed for the season and says it will publish its 2027 opening dates shortly.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Giardini Aphrodite Apollon, Sant'Angelo</h3>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Above the fishing village of Sant'Angelo on the south coast. It has twelve thermal pools between 20 and 40 degrees, a covered hydromassage pool, two seawater tubs and a sauna in a natural grotto, with a private beach a short walk or taxi boat away. In 2026 a full day cost about <strong>40 euros</strong> and a half day about 30. We could not confirm this year's closing date from the park itself, so call before going late in the season.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Terme di Cavascura, Maronti</h3>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The oldest and least polished of the lot. Cavascura sits in a ravine about 300 metres inland from <a href="/en-us/beaches/islands/spiaggia-maronti/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Maronti beach</a>, and its baths are individual tubs cut into the tuff, with a natural steam cave, warm mud and massage. Staff tell you the order and how long to stay in each. You reach it on foot along the ravine path from Maronti, or by boat from Sant'Angelo and then the walk. The season usually runs from late April to early November, from 8:30 to 18:00. Cavascura does not publish prices online, and the path and entrance are not suitable for people with reduced mobility.
    </p>

    <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-med-gold pl-6 my-8">
      <p class="text-slate-700 text-xl font-serif italic leading-relaxed">
        "Poseidon and Negombo are a day out. Cavascura is a bath."
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </div>

  <!-- Scrajo -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Vico Equense: The Sulphur Water of Scrajo</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Two kilometres north of the centre of Vico Equense, where the coast road drops towards Castellammare, sulphur springs come out of the limestone at the foot of the cliff. The Scrajo baths were founded in 1895, and they made Vico Equense a spa resort in the late nineteenth century, with treatments for skin and respiratory conditions. The Scala family has owned the springs for more than six generations.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Today the place works differently. The historic Liberty building of Scrajo Terme now presents itself mainly as a venue for weddings and events from April to October. Bathing happens below, at the <strong>Scrajo Mare 1899</strong> beach club on the SS145, where the sulphur water is channelled into a pool against the rock, next to the sea. The water is around <strong>18 degrees</strong>: this is a cool mineral dip, not a hot spring, and the smell of sulphur is part of the experience.
    </p>
    <ul class="space-y-3 mb-8">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Season.</strong> From around Easter to October, weather permitting, during the day. The restaurant upstairs serves lunch.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Prices.</strong> No price list is published online. Visitor reviews from summer 2026 describe it as expensive in high season, with one citing about 80 euros for an umbrella and two loungers. Call ahead.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Facilities.</strong> Loungers and umbrellas, changing cabins, hot and cold showers, snack bar, restaurant and private parking.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Ulysse -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Sorrento: The Ulysse Spa</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The "Ulisse" people mention in Sorrento is the Ulysse wellness centre on Via del Mare, in the same complex as the Ulisse Deluxe hotel. It is a four storey building of about 2,500 square metres with a gym, a semi Olympic indoor pool, a beauty centre and a spa. The spa circuit includes a large warm saltwater pool with hydromassage and coloured lighting, a steam grotto, a Finnish sauna, sensory showers, an ice grotto and a relaxation room with herbal teas.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Be clear about what it is: a modern indoor spa with heated salt water, not a natural spring. Its value is that it is in town, open to the public and not tied to the summer season. The spa sells its wellness circuit, alone or with massages, to visitors who are not staying at the hotel, and hotel guests get a discount. Listed opening hours are 9:00 to 20:00 Monday to Saturday and 10:00 to 19:00 on Sunday, but the operator warns that the spa and pool may close for maintenance at certain times of year. No prices are published online, so phone before you go.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Comparison -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Side by Side</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
      <table class="w-full text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-white">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left p-5 font-serif font-bold text-med-gold">Place</th>
            <th class="text-left p-5 font-serif font-bold text-med-gold">Season</th>
            <th class="text-left p-5 font-serif font-bold text-med-gold">Full day (2026)</th>
            <th class="text-left p-5 font-serif font-bold text-med-gold">Best for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="bg-white">
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Poseidon, Forio</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">18 April to 31 October</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">50 euros</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Variety, a long beach, late season visits</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Negombo, Lacco Ameno</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">23 April to 13 October</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">About 55 to 70 euros</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Gardens, a sandy bay, families with small children</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Castiglione, Casamicciola</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Spring to October, 2027 dates pending</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">About 28 euros</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Budget, quieter days</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Aphrodite Apollon, Sant'Angelo</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Thermal season, check dates</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">About 40 euros</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Combining with Sant'Angelo village</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Cavascura, Maronti</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Late April to early November</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Not published</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">History, a rustic bath, a beach day at Maronti</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Scrajo Mare, Vico Equense</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Easter to October</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Not published, beach club rates</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">A half day from Sorrento, a cool sulphur dip</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Ulysse, Sorrento</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Year round, with maintenance closures</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Not published</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Winter, rainy days, no travel</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Getting there -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Getting There from Sorrento and Positano</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-3 mb-6">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Sorrento to Ischia.</strong> Alilauro runs a direct hydrofoil from 1 April to 31 October, taking about an hour, with fares from around 24 euros one way. In 2026 it sailed in the morning on only some days of the week, so check the timetable and the return time before planning a day trip.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Positano to Ischia.</strong> Alicost runs a hydrofoil via Capri from June to September, and on weekends only in the shoulder months, with fares from around 28 euros. It is a long ride, better suited to a night or two on the island.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Out of season.</strong> Take the Circumvesuviana to Naples and a ferry or hydrofoil from there. Our <a href="/en-us/guide/ferries/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">ferries guide</a> has the routes.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>On Ischia.</strong> EAV buses circle the island: lines 1 and 2 and the two circular lines (CS and CD) stop about 500 metres from Negombo, and Poseidon is on the Citara road outside Forio. Taxis and microtaxis wait at the ports. For Cavascura, go down to Maronti or take the boat from Sant'Angelo.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Sorrento to Scrajo.</strong> The Circumvesuviana reaches Vico Equense in about 15 minutes. Scrajo is about 2 km further north: take a local bus or a taxi from the station, as the SS145 has no pavement. A summer only halt at Scrajo has operated on the line in the past, so check the EAV timetable. By car, Scrajo is on the SS145 between Castellammare and Vico Equense.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Ulysse.</strong> It is on Via del Mare in Sorrento, walkable from the centre for most hotels in town.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Which one -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Which One, and When</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      For a first thermal day with plenty of choice, Poseidon is the safe pick, and the only big park still open in late October. For gardens and a sandy beach with small children, Negombo. For a cheaper, calmer day, Castiglione or Aphrodite Apollon. For something older and stranger, Cavascura, combined with a swim at Maronti. If you are staying in Sorrento without a day to spare for the island, Scrajo is a half day trip in summer, and Ulysse is the answer in winter or on a rainy afternoon.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The best months for Ischia's parks are May, June, September and early October: the hot pools are more pleasant when the air is not, Negombo's low season prices apply, and the parks are quieter. July and August mean higher prices, crowded pools and the need to book. If you are weighing up a longer stay, our comparisons of <a href="/en-us/blog/capri-vs-ischia/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Capri and Ischia</a> and of <a href="/en-us/blog/procida-vs-ischia/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Procida and Ischia</a> will help.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Local Tips -->
  <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-xl border border-slate-100">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-6">
      <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-med-gold flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 18h6"/><path d="M10 22h4"/><path d="M15.09 14c.18-.98.65-1.74 1.41-2.5A4.65 4.61 0 0 0 18 8 6 6 0 0 0 6 8c0 1 .23 2.23 1.5 3.5A4.61 4.61 0 0 1 8.91 14"/></svg>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">Expert Tips</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Pack a swim cap and non slip sandals</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Caps are compulsory at Poseidon and expected at indoor pools such as Ulysse. The stone paths and steps in the parks are slippery when wet, so leave fashion sandals at the hotel. Bring a towel or robe, or budget for renting one, and some cash for deposits.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Go hot to cool, and not for too long</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Thermal water at 38 to 40 degrees is tiring. Short soaks, rests in the shade and plenty of water work better than an hour in the hottest pool. If you are pregnant or have heart or blood pressure problems, ask your doctor before a thermal day.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Use the afternoon ticket</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">If you arrive from Sorrento on the morning hydrofoil, you reach the park around midday anyway. Poseidon's afternoon ticket starts at 13:00 and costs less, which suits a day trip.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Check this year's figures</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">The prices and dates here are the 2026 ones. Parks publish the new season in spring, and hydrofoil timetables change during the year. Confirm both on the official sites before you book anything around them.</p>
      </div>
